WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.315
Hi, I'm Kelp!

00:00:02.315 --> 00:00:06.072
Jing turns key presses and prompts into first-person video, with sound.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.780
Here's what the keys drive.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:13.093
The idea: six numbers reach all fifty layers, through injectors that start

00:00:13.093 --> 00:00:16.181
at zero, so control trains into a finished model.

00:00:19.175 --> 00:00:21.283
Keys fill a six-slot camera vector.

00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:25.498
Since a September fix, four are live: forward, strafe, pitch and yaw.

00:00:25.498 --> 00:00:27.254
Rise and roll stay zero.

00:00:27.674 --> 00:00:31.337
A shared encoder lifts six numbers to five hundred and twelve.

00:00:31.337 --> 00:00:35.001
Fifty injectors, one per layer, turn that into scale and shift.

00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:39.459
Each modulates its layer's hidden state, in one trunk that carries

00:00:39.459 --> 00:00:40.927
prompt, video and audio.

00:00:40.927 --> 00:00:42.762
Even dialogue arrives as text.

00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:47.495
Scale and shift start at zero, so each injector does nothing at

00:00:47.495 --> 00:00:48.933
first: the ControlNet trick.

00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.808
About three hundred million parameters, under one percent.

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:56.462
Here it is in the project's own reel: one shared opening

00:00:56.462 --> 00:00:57.551
frame, at the top.

00:00:57.971 --> 00:01:01.167
Below, three action chunks: navigation, action, communication.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.450
Each steers its own continuation.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.103
Which checkpoint rendered it, the reel doesn't say.

00:01:09.025 --> 00:01:10.660
The repository gives the clock.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.682
One control slice is seventeen frames: point seven oh eight seconds, at

00:01:15.682 --> 00:01:17.215
twenty-four frames a second.

00:01:17.635 --> 00:01:18.831
Times the slices.

00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:23.216
The bakery example sums to twenty-one: three hundred and sixty-two frames.

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.161
Attention runs both ways over the whole clip.

00:01:27.161 --> 00:01:30.653
Frame one depends on the last, so it arrives with the last.

00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:34.251
A causal model, coming soon, could emit frames as it goes.

00:01:34.251 --> 00:01:37.139
Even then, a key press waits for the next slice.

00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:44.147
Behind six numbers sits a lot of model: fifty injectors, on a seventeen-frame clock.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.214
To recap: six numbers, fifty injectors that start at zero, and a seventeen-frame clock.

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:54.676
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:54.676 --> 00:01:55.259
I'm Kelp.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:55.551
Bye!
